{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":""The Hard Easy" is the twenty-third episode of the fourth season of the American animated television series "Adventure Time".","title":"The Hard Easy (Adventure Time)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The episode was written and storyboarded by Tom Herpich and Skyler Page, from a story by Patrick McHale, Kent Osborne, and Pendleton Ward.","title":"The Hard Easy (Adventure Time)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It originally aired on Cartoon Network on October 1, 2012.","title":"The Hard Easy (Adventure Time)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The episode guest stars Brian Doyle-Murray as Prince Huge and Jonathan Katz as the Mudscamp elder.","title":"The Hard Easy (Adventure Time)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Hard Easy is a 2006 American action crime drama film directed by Ari Ryan, and written by Jon Lindstrom and Tom Schanley.","title":"The Hard Easy (film)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film stars Henry Thomas, Vera Farmiga, David Boreanaz, Bruce Dern, Peter Weller, Gary Busey, and Nick Lachey.","title":"The Hard Easy (film)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film was released straight-to-DVD in the United States on May 22, 2007.","title":"The Hard Easy (film)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Evicted!"","title":"Evicted!"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" is the twelfth episode of the first season of the American animated television series "Adventure Time".","title":"Evicted!"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The episode was written and storyboarded by Bert Youn and Sean Jimenez, from a story by Adam Muto.","title":"Evicted!"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It originally aired on Cartoon Network on March 18, 2010 as a preview for the series; it later officially aired on May 17, 2010.","title":"Evicted!"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The episode guest stars Erik Estrada as King Worm.","title":"Evicted!"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The episode marks the first appearance of Marceline the Vampire Queen (voiced by Olivia Olson), who would go on to play a larger role in the series as a friend and companion to Finn and Jake.","title":"Evicted!"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Brother from Another Series" is the sixteenth episode of "The Simpsons"<nowiki>'</nowiki> eighth season.","title":"Brother from Another Series"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It originally aired on the Fox network in the United States on February 23, 1997.","title":"Brother from Another Series"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Sideshow Bob is released from prison into the care of his brother Cecil and claims to be a changed man.","title":"Brother from Another Series"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" However, Bart does not believe him and tries to find out what Bob is up to.","title":"Brother from Another Series"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" It was the first episode directed by Pete Michels and was written by Ken Keeler.","title":"Brother from Another Series"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The episode guest stars Kelsey Grammer in his sixth appearance as Sideshow Bob and David Hyde Pierce as Cecil.","title":"Brother from Another Series"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The title is not only a pun on the movie "The Brother from Another Planet" (used for a previous episode as well), but also a reference to the fact that guest stars Kelsey Grammer (Frasier Crane) and David Hyde Pierce (Niles Crane) also played bickering brothers on the NBC sitcom "Frasier".","title":"Brother from Another Series"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Traitor's Gate" is the fourth episode in the first series of the British television series "Spooks".","title":"Traitor's Gate (Spooks)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It first aired on BBC One in the United Kingdom on 4 June 2002, on a Tuesday; the other first series episodes aired on Mondays.","title":"Traitor's Gate (Spooks)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The episode was written by Howard Brenton, and directed by Rob Bailey.","title":"Traitor's Gate (Spooks)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The episode focuses on MI5 and 6's efforts in taking down a terrorist cell before they can succeed in attacking 43rd President of the United States George W. Bush.","title":"Traitor's Gate (Spooks)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The episode guest stars Anthony Head and Hugh Laurie, who play their respective characters Peter Salter and Jools Siviter.","title":"Traitor's Gate (Spooks)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" After its first broadcast, "Traitor's Gate" was seen by 6 million viewers, the lowest ratings in the first series.","title":"Traitor's Gate (Spooks)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Roy's Wedding" is the second episode of the ninth season of the American comedy television series "The Office" and the show's 178th episode overall.","title":"Roy's Wedding"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The episode originally aired on NBC on September 27, 2012.","title":"Roy's Wedding"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The episode guest stars David Denman as Roy Anderson, Michael Patrick McGill as his brother Kenny, Robert R. Shafer as Bob Vance, and Ameenah Kaplan as Val.","title":"Roy's Wedding"}],"peer_messages":["Evicted!"}
